---
intent: >
  Insert a structural null-args pre-check between the brain.aide parse step
  and the .mcp.json read in the existing detection pipeline so any null
  entry in `parseResult.mcpServerConfig.args` returns `no-mcp-entry`
  immediately, before the byte-for-byte drift comparison. Preserve the
  closed four-state vocabulary, preserve every other invariant
  (no-throw, no-stat, no-registry, no-name-dispatch, no-auto-sync,
  structural drift comparison), and add canonical detection-path tests
  that lock in the precedence ordering — including the matching-nulls
  regression case the rework exists to prevent.
---

## Project Structure

The detector remains a single-file orchestrator at this scope. The
existing nine-step pipeline grows to ten steps by inserting a Step 3.5
(renumbered to Step 4) that scans the parsed `mcpServerConfig.args` for
JS `null` and short-circuits to `no-mcp-entry`. No helper subfolders are
introduced; the new step is a single `.some(a => a === null)` predicate
plus a one-liner return — well below the ~30-line factor-out threshold
the prior plan named.

```
src/service/buildBrainState/
├── .aide                ← [existing, aligned] intent spec — read first; defines the null-args precedence rule
├── plan.aide            ← [this file]
├── todo.aide            ← [existing] prior-rework todo (all checked); a fresh todo will be authored by QA after build
├── index.ts             ← [modify] insert null-args pre-check between parse and read; renumber/expand JSDoc pipeline
│                           default export signature unchanged:
│                             default async function buildBrainState(
│                               root: string
│                             ): Promise<BrainState>
└── index.test.ts        ← [modify] add three canonical null-precedence test cases (3p, 3q, 3r);
                            no existing test changes shape
```

The default export stays a single positional `(root: string)` per the
spec's single-input rule. JSDoc on the export expands its numbered
pipeline narrative from nine steps to ten by inserting the null-args
check at the canonical pre-read position. Tier 3 inline comments narrate
the new step inside the orchestrator body alongside the existing
step-by-step inline narration.

## Prerequisites

This plan consumes one upstream contract that must ship before this
plan's steps run:

- **`src/service/parseBrainAide/`** must export `parseBrainAide` such
  that on `kind: "ok"`, `mcpServerConfig.args` is typed
  `(string | null)[]`. Bare `-` entries in the YAML `args` list parse
  to JS `null` and propagate through to consumers verbatim. The parser
  never substitutes nulls, never rejects null entries, never throws on
  null. The `interpolateArgs(config)` named export must accept a
  config whose `args` is `(string | null)[]` and return
  `(string | null)[]` — null entries pass through unchanged because
  `${...}` substitution applies only to strings.

  Concretely, `BrainAideMcpServerConfig` in `src/types/index.ts` must
  read:

  ```
  type BrainAideMcpServerConfig = {
    command: string;
    args: (string | null)[];
  };
  ```

  The detector's null pre-check (Step 2 below) depends on this typing:
  scanning `parseResult.mcpServerConfig.args` for JS `null` is only
  type-safe when the array element type admits `null`. If the
  prerequisite has not landed when this plan starts, halt and escalate
  — the null-args check cannot compile against a `string[]` element
  type without `as` casts that hide a real type contract.

  `parseBrainAide`'s `kind` discriminant remains the four-variant
  tagged union (`ok | missing | malformed-frontmatter | malformed-body`)
  unchanged in shape; only the `args` element type widens. No other
  field on the parser's `ok` payload changes.

This plan does NOT modify `src/types/`, `src/service/parseBrainAide/`,
or any consumer (`src/tools/info`, `src/tools/brain`, `src/cli/sync`).
Those modules' contracts are untouched by this rework — the detector's
return shape (the four-variant `BrainState`) is byte-identical to the
post-prior-rework shape, so consumer plans do not need to migrate.

## Plan

### 1. Insert the null-args pre-check into the orchestrator pipeline in `src/service/buildBrainState/index.ts`

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/services/error-handling`

- [ ] Locate the existing pipeline boundary in `src/service/buildBrainState/index.ts`
      between Step 3 (the `parseBrainAide` call and its `kind !== "ok"`
      branch) and Step 4 (the `readFile(mcpConfigPath, "utf-8")` try/catch).
      The new step is inserted as a structural check on the parsed
      config BEFORE any read of `.mcp.json`. Do NOT move existing steps
      around; the insertion is purely additive between two adjacent
      steps.

      The new step has three load-bearing properties, all dictated by
      the spec's `outcomes.desired[5]`, `outcomes.desired[6]`, the
      Strategy paragraphs "Structural detection over string-matching"
      and "Precedence ordering: parse → null-check → compare," and the
      retired-regression Bad example "Returning `ok` when both sides
      match byte-for-byte on null":

      - **Structural detection.** The check is
        `parseResult.mcpServerConfig.args.some(a => a === null)` —
        identity comparison against the JS value `null` produced by
        `parseBrainAide` when it encounters a bare `-` in YAML args.
        It is NOT pattern-matching on string contents, NOT comparison
        against the literal strings `"null"` or `"~"`, NOT a search
        for `<BRAIN_PATH>`-shape sentinels or any other in-band string
        marker. The spec's undesired outcomes call out each of those
        forbidden alternatives by name; the Read entries above
        describe the structural-detection invariant the predicate
        encodes.
      - **Precedence ordering.** The check runs BEFORE the
        `readFile(mcpConfigPath, ...)` call in the existing Step 4.
        That means: parse brain.aide → if any arg is null, return
        `no-mcp-entry` and STOP, never reading `.mcp.json` on the
        null-armed branch. This precedence is the entire point of
        the rework — running the drift comparison ahead of the null
        scan let two equally null-bearing files agree byte-for-byte
        and surface as `ok`. The new step short-circuits before that
        comparison can ever fire.
      - **Closed four-state vocabulary.** The return on the null
        branch is `{ status: "no-mcp-entry", name, hints }`. NO new
        status variant is introduced. NO `unwired`,
        `incomplete-args`, `placeholder-present`, `not-yet-configured`,
        or any rename of the same idea. The spec's closed-four-state
        invariant is non-negotiable — null-armed configs collapse
        semantically into `no-mcp-entry` because the brain's MCP
        entry is not yet wireable, which is exactly what
        `no-mcp-entry` already means.

      Concretely, after the existing `const { name, mcpServerConfig }
      = parseResult;` destructure (which lives at the bottom of the
      existing Step 3), insert one new pipeline step that does the
      following:

      - Scans `mcpServerConfig.args` for any element that is JS
        `null`, using `.some()` with the predicate `(a) => a === null`.
        The predicate is the literal three-character comparison
        `a === null`. Do not introduce a helper file or a named
        function — the predicate is one expression, the spec's "structural
        property of the parsed config" framing is already
        documented in the Tier 3 inline comment, and the
        orchestrator/helper rule does not extract one-liners.
      - On match (any null present), returns
        `{ status: "no-mcp-entry", name, hints }` — same shape the
        Step 4–6 branches return when `.mcp.json` is missing,
        unparseable, or has no `brain` key. Threading the parsed
        `name` keeps the remediation-prose contract intact: the
        orchestrator can name the wired brand even when the args
        list is unwired.
      - On no match, falls through to the existing Step 4 (which
        becomes Step 5 in the renumbered pipeline).

      Hard invariants the new step MUST preserve and the surrounding
      code must not regress:

      - **No throw.** The predicate is total over `(string | null)[]`;
        no path can throw. The detector's never-throws invariant
        carries through.
      - **No path stat.** No `existsSync`, `stat`, or `lstat` is
        added — the new step only reads JS values already in memory
        from `parseBrainAide`.
      - **No registry import.** Do not import from
        `@/service/brainBackends` or any retired-registry equivalent
        to "look up which arg slot is the path." The detector does
        not introspect arg semantics; it only checks for null
        presence.
      - **No name dispatch.** The predicate does not vary on
        `name`. It is the same predicate for `name: obsidian`,
        `name: notion`, and any other label.
      - **No legacy fallback.** The branch returns
        `no-mcp-entry`, not `mcp-drift`, not `ok`. No fallback to
        "compare anyway and call it drift" if `.mcp.json` happens
        to also have nulls.
      - **No body-section reads.** The predicate runs on
        `mcpServerConfig.args` only. Body sections (`orientation`,
        `config`, `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
        `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`) on the parser's `ok`
        result are not touched by this detector.

