### 3. Add canonical null-precedence test cases in `src/service/buildBrainState/index.test.ts`

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [ ] Add three new test cases to the existing test file. The cases
      land in their own `describe` blocks, named with continuation
      letters from the existing suite (the file currently runs cases
      3a–3o; the new cases are 3p, 3q, 3r). The new cases do NOT
      modify any existing case. The fixture-helper landscape needs
      one extension before the cases land:

      - `makeBrainAideContent` currently accepts `args?: string[]`.
        Widen the parameter type to `args?: (string | null)[]` so
        tests can pass YAML-null-shaped fixtures through the helper
        without `as` casts. The helper currently emits each arg as
        `    - "${a}"` — that template will not work for null
        entries (a JS `null` string-interpolated produces the literal
        string `"null"`, which is a string the parser would parse
        as the string `"null"`, not the YAML null we need). Update
        the args-emit logic to branch: when the arg is JS `null`,
        emit `    -` (a bare dash with no scalar after it — the
        YAML form that parses to JS `null` per the parseBrainAide
        contract); when the arg is a string, emit
        `    - "<value>"` as before. Confirm by reading the
        rewritten helper end-to-end that the emitted YAML is
        parseable and that null entries round-trip through
        `parseBrainAide` to JS `null`. A concrete validation: a
        fixture call `makeBrainAideContent({ args: ["a", null, "b"] })`
        must produce a YAML body whose `args` list, when parsed,
        yields exactly `["a", null, "b"]`.

      The three new test cases:

      - **3p. Null-armed brain.aide with no `.mcp.json` returns
        `no-mcp-entry` (anchors the basic null-detection branch).**
        Write a brain.aide via `makeBrainAideContent` whose args
        contain at least one JS `null` at a non-edge position (e.g.
        `args: ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", null]`). Do not write a
        `.mcp.json`. Call `buildBrainState(tempRoot)` and assert:
        - `result.status === "no-mcp-entry"`
        - `result.name === "obsidian"` (threaded through from
          brain.aide)
        - `result.hints` matches the mocked hints array (empty by
          default, populated when the suite-level mock is overridden)
        - The result has NO `rootPath`, `connector`, `entryFile`,
          `tools`, or `backend` fields (the same retired-fields
          assertions the other cases carry, kept for invariant
          consistency)

      - **3q. Null-armed brain.aide with a matching null-armed
        `.mcp.json` returns `no-mcp-entry`, NOT `ok` (the canonical
        regression-prevention case).** This is the test that locks
        in the entire rework's correctness. Write a brain.aide via
        `makeBrainAideContent` whose args contain a JS `null` (e.g.
        `args: ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", null]`). Write a `.mcp.json`
        whose `mcpServers.brain.args` is byte-identical to the
        brain.aide-derived array — i.e. it ALSO has a JSON null at
        the same position (`{ "command": "npx", "args":
        ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", null] }`). The two files agree
        byte-for-byte — the structural drift comparison from the
        existing Step 9 would return `ok` if it were allowed to
        run. The null pre-check from Step 4 must short-circuit
        before that comparison fires. Assert:
        - `result.status === "no-mcp-entry"` — NEVER `ok`, NEVER
          `mcp-drift`.
        - `result.name === "obsidian"`.
        - The result carries `hints`.

        Add an inline comment above the assertion block explaining
        why this case is load-bearing: it is the exact regression
        the rework exists to prevent. The comment should reference
        the spec's "Bad examples" entry "Returning `ok` when both
        sides match byte-for-byte on null." Without this test, a
        future contributor moving the null check below the
        `.mcp.json` read would silently regress the detector and
        the suite would still go green; this test makes that
        regression a hard failure.

        Note for the implementor: writing a `.mcp.json` with a
        JSON null is straightforward — `JSON.stringify({
        mcpServers: { brain: { command: "npx", args:
        ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", null] } } }, null, 2)` produces the
        expected file contents. The existing `makeMcpJson` helper
        accepts `args: string[]`; widen it to
        `(string | null)[]` so this case can use the helper. (The
        widening is type-only; `JSON.stringify` already serializes
        JS `null` to JSON null without modification.)

      - **3r. Non-null brain.aide with matching `.mcp.json` returns
        `ok` (anchors the negative case — null pre-check does not
        fire when no nulls are present).** This case anchors the
        invariant that the new step does not over-fire. The null
        pre-check returning `no-mcp-entry` only on actual nulls is
        load-bearing — a buggy implementation that returned
        `no-mcp-entry` on every config (or that mistook empty
        strings, the literal string `"null"`, or the literal
        string `"~"` for nulls) would also pass case 3p but would
        regress case 3a (the existing happy-path test). The 3r
        case is a deliberate restatement of the happy path with
        ALL string args (no nulls anywhere) to make the invariant
        explicit in the new test block. Assert:
        - `result.status === "ok"` — the null pre-check does NOT
          fire on a fully-wired config.
        - `result.name === "obsidian"`.
        - The result carries `hints`.

        Optionally extend with `it.each` rows that vary the args
        list shape (single arg, three args, args containing the
        literal STRING `"null"` to anchor the spec's "string-
        equality with `'null'`" Bad example — that string is NOT
        JS null and must NOT trigger the pre-check). The
        literal-string-`"null"` row is the most surgical
        regression test against the wrong predicate; if a future
        implementor "simplifies" the predicate to
        `a == null` (loose equality, which would still match JS
        null but NOT the string `"null"`) the suite stays green.
        Only an implementation that switched to
        `a === "null" || a === "~"` — the spec's named anti-pattern
        — would regress, and the row catches that.

      Test conventions to mirror across all three new cases (drawn
      from the existing suite's idioms):

      - Each case lives in its own top-level `describe` block named
        with the suite-letter pattern (`describe("buildBrainState
        — null-armed args: <subject> (3p)", () => { ... })`).
      - Setup uses the same `beforeEach`/`afterEach` temp-dir
        pattern the rest of the file uses; no new fixture
        infrastructure is introduced beyond the helper widening
        above.
      - Each case calls `buildBrainState(tempRoot)` exactly once
        and asserts on the returned `BrainState`. No probing into
        internal state or import-mocking of `parseBrainAide`
        beyond what the existing suite already does (the existing
        suite mocks `detectFramework` and `resolveBrainHints` and
        runs the real parser against real temp files; the new
        cases use the same shape).
      - No new mock setup. The new cases inherit the file's
        existing `vi.mock("@/service/install/detectFramework/index.js")`
        and `vi.mock("@/service/install/resolveBrainHints/index.js")`
        declarations and the existing
        `mockDetectFramework.mockResolvedValue(FIXED_FRAMEWORK_CONFIG)`
        / `mockResolveBrainHints.mockResolvedValue([])` setup
        in `beforeEach`.

      Confirm by reading the file end-to-end after the new cases
      land that no obsolete `<vault-path>` placeholder string is
      reintroduced into any fixture — the spec's undesired
      outcomes forbid pattern-matching string sentinels, and the
      tests must not seed a sentinel-shaped fixture that a
      regression-prone implementor might be tempted to recognize.
      The `makeBrainAideContent` helper currently has a default of
      `args = ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "<vault-path>"]` — the
      `<vault-path>` literal is just a non-empty string the
      existing suite uses as a stand-in for an inline path; it is
      NOT a sentinel the detector recognizes, and removing or
      replacing it is OUT OF SCOPE for this plan.

