- [ ] Sanity grep `src/service/buildBrainState/`:

      - Confirm exactly one `.some(a => a === null)` (or equivalent
        identity-against-null predicate) appears in `index.ts`. More
        than one is a duplicate-detection regression; zero is the
        rework not landing.
      - Confirm zero references to `<BRAIN_PATH>`, `<API_TOKEN>`,
        the literal string `"null"` as a comparison RHS, the literal
        string `"~"` as a comparison RHS, `existsSync`, `stat`,
        `lstat`, `invalid-path`, `connector`, `rootPath`,
        `entryFile`, `tools` (as a brain.aide field), `backend`,
        `resolveBackend`, `brainBackends`, `getDriverById`. Each is
        a regression tripwire from the spec's undesired outcomes.
      - Confirm zero references to a fifth status state name —
        `unwired`, `incomplete-args`, `placeholder-present`,
        `not-yet-configured`. Any hit is a closed-vocabulary
        violation.

## Decisions

- **Structural detection over string-matching.** The predicate is
  `args.some(a => a === null)` — JS identity comparison against the
  value `parseBrainAide` actually produces when it parses a bare `-`
  in YAML args. The retired sentinel design (`<BRAIN_PATH>`,
  `<API_TOKEN>`, magic UUIDs, angle-bracket-wrapped tokens) failed
  in production because literal placeholder strings propagated
  byte-for-byte through both files and the drift comparison
  returned `ok`; the brain MCP server then failed silently at
  launch. The structural fix moves detection to the language level:
  YAML null is the unambiguous unwired-slot signal, the parser
  preserves it as JS `null`, and the detector recognizes it via
  `=== null`. No in-band string vocabulary the package has to keep
  in sync with whatever templates and integrations ship — and no
  attack surface where a user's legitimate path string accidentally
  matches a sentinel pattern.

- **Null pre-check runs BEFORE the `.mcp.json` read.** This is the
  load-bearing decision the entire rework exists to enforce. The
  retired regression: a freshly-installed null-armed brain.aide and
  a sync-derived null-armed `.mcp.json` agreed byte-for-byte at the
  null position, the structural drift comparison saw equal-shaped
  args at every index, and the detector returned `ok`. Boot probe
  reported a healthy brain; brain MCP server failed silently. The
  precedence ordering — parse → null-check → compare — guarantees
  any null-armed config returns `no-mcp-entry` regardless of how
  clean the drift comparison would otherwise come back. Bytes that
  agree on null have agreed on broken state; the detector must
  surface the broken state, not the agreement.

- **No fifth status state.** A null-armed config maps semantically
  into `no-mcp-entry` rather than forking `unwired`,
  `incomplete-args`, `placeholder-present`, or
  `not-yet-configured`. The spec's `outcomes.undesired[5]` rejects
  every variant of a fifth state by name. Rationale: the user's
  remediation is identical across the two sub-cases of "the brain's
  MCP entry is not yet wired" — whether `.mcp.json` is missing the
  key or `mcpServerConfig.args` is null-armed, the recovery is to
  run `/aide:brain config` to fill the slot, then `npx aidemd-mcp
  sync` to write the entry. A fifth status would force every
  consumer of the four-state union (boot probe prose composer,
  runtime brain tool response shape, orchestrator remediation
  routing) to grow another branch with identical remediation —
  pure cost, no benefit.

- **Renumber the JSDoc pipeline (10 steps), don't sub-number (3.5).**
  The existing JSDoc enumerates flat integers; sub-numbering
  (3.5, 4a) signals the pipeline grew unprincipled. Renumbering
  every step past the insertion site keeps the narrative
  uniform — every step has an integer, the new step is "Step 4,"
  and the formerly-Step-9 happy path becomes "Step 10." The
  inline orchestrator comments narrate the new step at the
  insertion site so a reader walking the body file
  top-to-bottom encounters the rationale where the code lives,
  not just in the JSDoc above the export.

- **No helper subfolder for the new step.** The check is a
  single `.some()` call with a one-line predicate; the
  orchestrator/helper rule extracts when a helper "supports an
  orchestrator," not for one-line predicates. The Tier 3 inline
  comment above the predicate carries the rationale (why
  structural, why before the read), and the JSDoc pipeline
  bullet carries the narrative position. Factoring this into
  `src/service/buildBrainState/checkNullArgs/` would invent
  ceremony for no testability or readability gain — the
  predicate is already trivially testable as part of the
  end-to-end orchestrator tests in 3p, 3q, and 3r.

- **Test-helper widening lives in this plan, not in a separate
  test-infra refactor.** `makeBrainAideContent` and `makeMcpJson`
  in the test file currently type their `args` parameter as
  `string[]`. Widening to `(string | null)[]` is necessary for
  the new cases to land without `as` casts that hide a real
  type contract. The widening is small (two parameter type
  changes plus the YAML-null emit branch in
  `makeBrainAideContent`), tightly coupled to the new test
  cases that depend on it, and lives entirely inside this
  module's `index.test.ts`. It does not belong in a separate
  plan — it would be churn for no scope-isolation gain. The
  widening is treated as fixture-infra setup for the new
  cases and lives inside Step 3.

- **3q is the canonical regression test; 3p and 3r anchor the
  surrounding invariants.** The matching-nulls case (3q) is the
  exact regression the rework exists to prevent — it is the
  single most load-bearing test in the new suite and the
  reason the precedence ordering is non-negotiable. 3p
  (null-armed brain.aide with no `.mcp.json`) anchors that the
  null pre-check fires before the read on the simpler branch
  too. 3r (non-null brain.aide with matching `.mcp.json`)
  anchors that the new step does NOT over-fire — a buggy
  predicate that returned `no-mcp-entry` on every config
  would still pass 3p and 3q but would regress 3r. The
  `it.each` row testing the literal STRING `"null"` (within
  3r) anchors the spec's named anti-pattern: comparing args
  to the string `"null"` instead of JS `null` would silently
  miss every actual unwired slot AND mis-fire on user content
  that happened to be the string `"null"` — the row catches
  that exact substitution.

- **Detection-path tests use real temp dirs and real
  brain.aide / `.mcp.json` files (no fs mocks).** The
  detector's I/O surface is two `readFile` calls; mocking
  either would test the mock more than the detector. The
  existing test file already follows this convention — the
  new cases inherit the convention without modification. The
  null branch tests in particular benefit: a real
  `parseBrainAide` parsing a real fixture confirms the YAML
  null actually round-trips to JS null end-to-end, which
  would be invisible to a parser-mocked test that
  hand-constructed the parser result.

- **No path stat, no fifth state, no sentinel matching, no
  registry import — every existing invariant carries through
  unchanged.** The rework is purely additive: one new step
  inserted at a fixed position, one new branch on the
  existing `no-mcp-entry` status. The detector's contract
  with consumers (`BrainState` shape, `name`-as-metadata,
  no-throw, no-stat, no-auto-sync, structural-drift-equality,
  pipeline-order-fixed) is byte-identical post-rework. The
  Step 4 sanity grep verifies the no-regression rule by
  scanning for every retired anti-pattern token and
  confirming none reappeared.

- **Plan scope is buildBrainState's module only.** The
  prerequisite parseBrainAide broadening is declared in the
  Prerequisites section but executed by that module's own
  plan, not by this one. Consumer modules (`src/tools/info`,
  `src/tools/brain`, `src/cli/sync`,
  `src/service/install/provisionBrain`) that consume the
  parser's broadened `(string | null)[]` typing have their
  own plans for migration; this plan does not touch them.
  Step 4's `tsc` log captures any cross-module errors so the
  orchestrator can verify dependency plans land in order.
